        "problem": "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{1}{2x}$ for $x\\in [1,2].$",
        "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{1}{2x},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{2{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{2{x}^{2}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{2{x}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{8}-2{x}^{4}+1}{4{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}-2{x}^{4}+1}{4{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{8}+2{x}^{4}+1}{4{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{4}+1)^{2}}{4{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+1}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-2})$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{-1}]|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}-{2}^{-1})-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-{1}^{-1})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
